WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.635
We've got the biggest
dreams of putting

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:06.205
our eyes on other worlds,
traveling to them,

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.173
making them our home.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.309
But how do we get there?

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.579
The stars are so far apart.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.447
We would need sailing
ships that could

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:19.185
sustain human crews over the
longest haul of all time.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:22.788
The nearest star is
four light years away.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:26.860
That's 24 trillion miles
to Proxima Centauri.

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:35.935
Just to give you some
idea of how far away

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.204
that point of light really is.

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:41.040
If NASA's Voyager
1 spacecraft, which

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:42.308
moves at a pretty good clip--

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.611
38,000 miles an hour--

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:49.282
was headed for Proxima Centauri,
it would take 70,000 years

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:52.017
to get there and
that's only the nearest

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:56.956
star out of the hundreds of
billions in our galaxy alone.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:01.394
[music playing]

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.336
So if we want to
endure as a species

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:14.073
beyond the projected shelf
life of our own planet,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.709
we'd better act like
the Polynesians.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.179
We need to take what
we know of nature

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:23.116
and build sailing ships
that can ride the light

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:25.218
as they once rode the wind.

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:34.260
These sails are
enormous, miles high,

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:37.130
but they're very
thin, 1,000 times

00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:39.032
thinner than a garbage bag.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:43.169
[music playing]

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:59.285
When a photon of light strikes
those magnificent sails,

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:00.887
it gives them a little push.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:04.857
[music playing]

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:15.134
This means that in
the vacuum of space

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.769
even the tiniest
push from a photon

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:20.572
will propel them ever
faster until they're

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.577
moving at a significant
fraction of the speed of light.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:28.548
[music playing]

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:34.821
When you get too far from your
star and the light dwindles,

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:37.257
lasers can do the trick.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:41.427
[music playing]

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:59.779
If we were to lightsail our
way to Proxima Centauri,

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:03.650
it wouldn't take 70,000
years, but only 20 years.

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:11.457
[music playing]

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:15.562
Proxima B lies in the
habitable zone of its star,

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:19.332
but we don't yet know if
it could support life.

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.268
Does it have a kind of
protective magnetic field

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:24.170
that has sheltered
the evolution of life

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:25.605
on the surface of our world?

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:33.079
Another consequence of Proxima
B's close location to its star

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:37.183
is that the planet is
probably tidally locked,

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.453
one side perpetually
facing the star,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.256
the other doomed
to endless night.

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:47.160
[music playing]

